#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116110} :activity/id #uuid "d07d2d5c-d3e0-595b-84ff-e18597a3d040" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116491 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n4\\. The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n5\\. The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "5a0ce34c-f39a-5c24-bab3-840ff974402c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3050 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "836cc4ee-5cd5-58a8-8fc6-487e670dc062" :step/math-academy-id 16471 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115065 :question/id #uuid "c0c08150-98f9-5677-a7a8-390cffc0c5b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4511"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050944}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567077}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"} :step/id #uuid "33db29bf-42ca-5174-b04b-c5529dc2bc98" :step/math-academy-id 16472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116112}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116112 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115066 :question/id #uuid "8c1e5afa-0afa-56ee-bbfa-4e84c64093ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4512"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567100}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "2a71d8e7-c420-59a3-927f-619b1fbcc37e" :step/math-academy-id 16473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115067 :question/id #uuid "cb19dcff-bb9d-55dc-941b-48153ca62e7a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4513"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567034}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"} :step/id #uuid "72cfa9ea-e123-5f2c-bad3-38a7904ca9f0" :step/math-academy-id 16474}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115476}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495176} :activity/id #uuid "bf58cc30-c0d8-5d47-9482-24ba16b9e39f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495176 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f00f19b1-a5d9-5f3f-892f-2e3d8e66d32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450566} {:answer/id #uuid "f24b70da-3d6e-5768-987e-5a6a1111b77e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450567}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450566} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac17f050-593c-55c0-be4c-979e6666933f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451237} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c3344c-65d7-58be-ae26-42bb9b9f586a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450568} {:answer/id #uuid "740835e0-32e5-50d4-b9e7-dfc4013e2ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450569}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450569} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e7cf2e-0dcc-5b45-a214-fc4802e94260" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451238} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9e3557-049b-5e3a-bce9-8906dde445a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450570} {:answer/id #uuid "9efcb6d2-5f9b-556f-ba0b-a54c998fb8e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450570} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd7516a-09ae-5918-99f5-dc75f2fb3320" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b252a44d-8417-5bdc-8a9b-ad6e81276b7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36075" :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has the unique  solution $⟨0,0⟩$\n- **III.** $⟨5,-4⟩$ is a solution to $Ax=0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. Again,  according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} :step/id #uuid "028bab25-3fed-5252-aa96-72f7de3058fa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dac26d2-ebbe-5a1e-83fa-4b3b6d5b4e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494448} {:answer/id #uuid "551b68dd-eb85-5e01-8e30-fd3cc7694f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494491} {:answer/id #uuid "939de576-f6fe-534e-bcc7-b91eb70d6b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494656} {:answer/id #uuid "c357f7bd-3a09-51e9-abea-8dc1deeb12c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494778} {:answer/id #uuid "efaad133-59c6-5b2b-9d22-976c341550bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494448} :answer-field/id #uuid "273b6a9c-3dfc-5014-9117-506724cb00ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11aeb2f0-20f1-51d5-8c52-39a1558cd757" :question/math-academy-id "q-47651" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines $y-x=1$ and $x+ky=4$, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point, then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nI. the lines $y-x=0$ and $x+ky=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. the lines $y-x=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n\nIII. $det\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that if we set \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n  \n\nthen the fourth part of the theorem is true. Hence,  all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III give us a true statement: \n\n- *If the lines $y-x=1$ and $x+ky=4$, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point, then $det\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "59b4b15c-df1d-52b3-a1d2-1425a0066736" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495224}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495220 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef111d9f-980c-5b03-9930-80f18435b245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472580} {:answer/id #uuid "a6862f2d-2762-5b0e-8f3a-1253e37655d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472581} :answer-field/id #uuid "5faeea23-e6b1-5b91-87f6-7b0a4fef3dcc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473685} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aefd83a-6b4c-56c7-b747-345c373d9b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472582} {:answer/id #uuid "24c81c04-8905-5c94-ac90-a70db567f3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472583}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472583} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2bd5816-6d9d-5da2-8f3f-64ef8acdb893" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473686} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6738bb8-1870-5396-9a90-bba160d497e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472584} {:answer/id #uuid "e7fc74ba-8f62-5436-9d2e-3c858d0578e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472584} :answer-field/id #uuid "7584f499-906d-554c-842b-3985b246d9ba" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2d9404d-2d42-56c6-b91b-5351ccec57cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-128139" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\nII. $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution\nIII. $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is invertible, $\\operatorname{det}(A)\\ne 0.$ Also, for each vector $b,$ the equation $Ax=b$ has the unique solution $x=A^{-1}b.$ In particular, $Ax=0$ has only the zero solution. Thus statements I and II are true and statement III is false. The correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "882122b9-54f2-5353-858e-8a741a0617ea"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495224 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dbd8a41-022e-505a-9a23-5897ee2615d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453946} {:answer/id #uuid "e964dc82-e823-526c-bb4e-d9d86b715c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453946} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5d2420-05be-505d-a6da-9370564b875a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454723} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bfaf3e3-c8c3-5b0f-905a-7cfc3c188946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453948} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7b77cf-0808-52a0-92c8-aef31c85857a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453949} :answer-field/id #uuid "b912caf2-b2fa-5b75-b5a5-849cbfd33c40" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "581a4ee7-2ef6-5d6a-8a10-f2e80e510c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453950} {:answer/id #uuid "b85c6ec3-9718-5d84-925f-86b8f81442df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453950} :answer-field/id #uuid "74197001-61cf-522e-acb4-234d12a1ff07" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aafb0544-5c12-5527-bc2a-2417215aad99" :question/math-academy-id "q-73566" :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is **not** invertible\n- **II.** The lines $2x-3y=1$ and $2x-2y=2$ intersect at exactly one point\n- **III.** $Ax=0$ has no solution" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false since, according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} :step/id #uuid "8ca0b9cc-1fcf-55ba-84e5-387e60cec625" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495228}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495228 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e722ed3-73e4-52c0-a421-554b9e9509ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451603} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8c1a33-14d5-5cc1-8cc8-cfa132ee5905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451603} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f67ecc6-4e68-5ff5-857f-036ed453fead" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6115e85e-49af-5de4-9017-fbc84811fcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451605} {:answer/id #uuid "eaebde56-3eac-5bbe-8241-b7a00be555c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451605} :answer-field/id #uuid "9389fb0c-e8ff-5dd4-bcab-a96185abbeb4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452650} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa797b64-c1e0-5eb2-85fb-866fba1ac56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451607} {:answer/id #uuid "896ae4a2-9a84-5c01-a7e8-af08641f9223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451608} :answer-field/id #uuid "39b24a35-e5b9-5f93-a09d-4ba2dac53cd5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "946ddb30-e9a3-52e2-ad46-5aa2c1f15eef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73567" :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** The lines $2x+2y=2$ and $3x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n- **III.** $Ax=0$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2k-6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- Statement III is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statement III is true."} :step/id #uuid "96da7574-bc62-58b8-af70-6d04a234aff0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495220}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494315}]}
